    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter June 27th with a high-ceiling core, led by **Junior Caminero** (67.96), whose 3-hole positioning against J. Cabrera provides our most stable path to R/RBI production. **Kazuma Okamoto** (64.53) offers the best individual matchup profile of the day, as a 4.81 xERA from Quantrill allows him to leverage elite power despite a neutral right-on-right platoon environment. The most efficient deployment for our SB-heavy strategy is **Casey Schmitt** (63.11), who benefits from a rare trifecta: a favorable 2-hole spot, a pitcher with a slow delivery boosting his stolen base probability, and wind-out conditions at the park that inflate his slugging floor. **Trea Turner** (62.50) remains a lock for the leadoff spot; while facing Scott is no walk in the park, his elite sprint speed makes him an auto-start regardless of matchup friction.\n\nThe most difficult benching decision involved **Carson Benge** (37.87), who hovered near our 40.0 floor but was ultimately sidelined due to a negative platoon split against Mayza and a challenging 3.17 xERA. While **Carson Benge** provides some utility, he did not clear the efficiency threshold required to displace our current starters. We are continuing to monitor **Yordan Alvarez** and **Christian Yelich**, both of whom remain unavailable for today's slate, and **Hyeseong Kim** remains stashed in the minors. Risk management is the theme for **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (47.62); while his speed is vital, he is walking into a suboptimal matchup against Harrison and must contend with a 15% drop-off due to the lefty-lefty dynamic, placing him on the lower end of our active rotation. We are banking on **Kyle Schwarber** (59.34) to provide the necessary power surge to offset the volatility of our lower-scoring middle infielders.",
